WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.480
A warm welcome to Björn Ulvaeus!

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:24.040
Did you notice how fat I was
on those pictures from Brighton?

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.240
From Brighton?
But you were thin as a line.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.360
-Later on, yes, but back then?
-I've never thought about that.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.240
-But I was.
-You were thin during the ABBA-years.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.640
Not at the very beginning.
That was later on.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.080
When we got home from Brighton,
after our victory...

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:50.720
I looked at myself in the mirror: "You
can't be a pop idol and look like that."

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.160
-ABBA became a sort of diet for you?
-Yes. And an efficient one at that.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.600
-But then you got really thin.
-Right.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.440
Was it because of stress or...?

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.720
No, no. There was this agreement
back then-

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.640
-that pop stars should
be thin as a thread.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.080
-And still is today, I believe.
-Absolutely.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.520
Were you on a special diet and stuff?

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.560
Well...

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.640
I don't really remember what I ate,
but I only ate a little.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.760
But there was nothing else going on? 
You weren't smoking or anything?

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.160
No. It was easier for our English
and American colleagues-

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.800
-because they had access
to some very nice drug-

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.000
-that made it easier for them not to eat.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.640
But we were those safe Swedes,
who...

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.680
Two Swedish couples.
We weren't into those things...

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.440
But...

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:03.080
Having you here as a guest, one feels
like reminiscing ABBA and things.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.360
But your memory isn't all that good,
is it?

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.320
It isn't, no.
At least not the episodic memory.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.600
That memory that I envy
so many people-

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.200
-when you can go back in time
and feel how it felt-

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:24.320
-almost feel the smell of that memory.
I don't have that.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.560
But that aside,
my memory is perfectly fine.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.080
I talked about this in my radio program
a couple of years ago.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.560
And this has grown, ever since,
on the internet, into:

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.440
"He suffers
from a serious memory loss."

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.960
And this has pursued me.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.480
But it only concerns
that specific episodic memory.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.840
-That emotional memory.
-Exactly.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.320
-Do you have that type of memory?
-I guess I could go back...

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.720
I'm trying here. No, I can't.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:04.160
Well, a little. A fragrance can
make me feel nostalgic.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.320
No, but I mean - when you're able
to reenact a scene in your mind-

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.480
-and then feel the way you felt back
then, the odours, etc, etc.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.600
But do you ever get nostalgic -
when we talk about ABBA, for instance?

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.360
No, I don't, actually. I wasn't...

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.240
Every one else is...

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:31.200
I don't wish I were still up on stage,
like Mick Jagger.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.760
It was good while it lasted, so...

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.200
But I do look back on that time with joy.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.560
But would you be able to,
theoretically speaking?

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.640
I don't think so.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:52.520
If you asked Benny he'd say: "No!"

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.560
But do you have
one more "Mama Mia" in you?

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.640
Would you be able to write
"Dancing Queen" today?

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:05.320
I believe that the best pop music
is written by young people-

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:09.880
-when they're at the summit
of their creativity.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:15.560
We all have such a period in our lives.
It only lasts about ten years.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.600
Then you enter another kind
of creativity.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:25.400
But we had that peak in question during
the 70's with ABBA.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.480
But it's not as if you're resting
on your laurels, is it?

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.280
-Do you have that expression?
-"Lagrar", yes.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.280
"Lagrar" in Swedish.
Because you're not, right?

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.120
-It depends on what you mean by that.
-You're very active.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:50.040
You're a debater. You write columns.
And you're into all kinds of...

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:53.400
But that stuff doesn't take up too much
of my time.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:58.200
-You are resting on your laurels, then?
-Yes, I suppose I am.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:05.320
I'm privileged enough to do
whatever I'm interested in-

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:12.280
-and stimulates me. Stressing and
chasing things is pointless to me now.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:22.120
-So, you don't need an alarm clock.
-Not really, no.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:27.160
I try to set my meetings
at around 11 o'clock.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.280
-What about you?
-Well, I have an alarm clock...

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:41.760
But I have someone who keeps waking me
up in the middle of the night, so...

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.240
-I have four.
-Four alarm clocks?

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.920
-I had four of them, yes.
-Yes, that's how it goes.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:58.880
Have you... You're interested
in various topics-

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.920
-that have nothing to do with Abba
or music.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.240
-Like most people.
-Yes. Societal issues.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.200
And one of these themes is atheism.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:13.000
-Like...
-Religion.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:20.840
You're one of the co-publishers
of one of our next guests.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.280
Where do you find the energy?

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.680
The person who did not get upset
by 9/11 is not born.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:36.640
That's when I thought: "My god."

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.760
"Religion is on its way in again."

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:47.720
To a forum where it does not belong -
where we legislate, etc.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.720
It was so sad, because it wasn't just
the islamists.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.440
There was a crusader
on the other side of the Atlantic.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.080
So, the whole situation turned
into this.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.160
It felt like:
"Oh, it's coming back again."

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:09.760
All these centuries
after the Enlightenment-

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:15.720
-where we've been working so hard to
reach this secularized society of our's.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:21.160
It was threatened. That's how it felt.
I can see now that this never happened.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.120
But that's when my commitment started.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:29.480
Is an atheist - as some people claim -
a person-

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:34.800
-who's just not been in a situation
where they've felt-

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.920
-that it would've been normal to turn
towards God?

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:44.200
I could imagine myself ending up
in such a situation-

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:49.880
-where my yearning for solace or help-

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:56.560
-gets so strong that I find myself
thinking: "Okay" - and give in.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.400
But I'm not saying that there is no God,
because I don't know.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:07.760
I'm just saying that I haven't found
sufficient reasons to believe otherwise.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.960
Would you be able to pray?

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.960
If someone convinced me that there does
exist something to pray to - yes.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.200
-What would you do...
-It's a difficult question.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:28.880
Yes, but what... You must've looked
for solace at one point or another?

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:37.240
If my wife can't give me solace
in that situation-

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:41.640
-then I'll have to move on.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:45.920
There's nothing else you can do.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.840
And it works.
With time it all works out.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.680
You would only turn to your wife
for solace?

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:57.840
Yes, she's the closest to thing
I would turn to.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.880
But about this solace... People's need
for solace being so great-

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:07.440
-does not mean that there's an answer to
that solace-

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.680
-or that there is someone
who can solace you.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.720
It only means that you have this strong
longing for solace-

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:18.440
-and for life not to end after death.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:23.040
Is it...

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.560
Are you saying that you don't believe
in solace?

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.720
I think we should take responsibility
for our own lives.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:37.160
And we only live once. And we should
take full responsibility for that.

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:47.400
But there's a lot of solace to be found
in a great song.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:51.520
Yes, music offers a lot of solace.
That's a good way.

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:56.640
It's been great having you here.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:01.760
We're going to meet one of your great
role models - dr Richard Dawkins.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:05.160
He's a very famous atheist.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:11.360
Please, welcome Agnetha Fältskog!

